{"turn":1,"elapsedMs":43454,"answer":"**No—tocilizumab did not establish efficacy in either diagnosis.** Both trials were randomized, double-blind and placebo-controlled. [S1][S2]\n\n- **Depression (2026):** **30 randomized; 29 received an infusion.** This inflammation-enriched proof-of-concept trial tested one infusion. The primary day-14 somatic-symptom outcome was null: adjusted difference −0.12 (95% CI −2.51 to 2.28). Later favourable trends and descriptive response/remission differences remain exploratory. Limitations include the small, selected sample and only four weeks’ follow-up. [S2]\n\n- **Schizophrenia (2018):** **36 adults** with stable, chronically symptomatic illness; three monthly infusions versus placebo. No benefit emerged for symptoms or cognition. CRP decreased while circulating IL-6 increased—biological effects without demonstrated clinical improvement. The small, chronic-illness sample leaves effects in early or inflammation-selected psychosis unresolved. [S1]\n\n**Source limits:** Depression appraisal included selected full-text passages; schizophrenia appraisal relied on the indexed abstract without comprehensive full-text methods review. Neither trial establishes sustained benefit, and these findings do not rule out benefit in every possible subgroup. [S1][S2]","chargedUpperUSD":0.8764124999999998}
{"turn":2,"elapsedMs":67995,"answer":"**Only limited evidence is hypomania-specific—not the broader bipolar evidence.** [S1]\n\n- **Bipolar depression:** depressive episodes in bipolar I or II. Trials show mixed results: pentoxifylline lowered IL-6 without improving depression more than placebo overall. Bipolar II depression is not hypomania. [S2][S1]\n- **Mania:** marked mood elevation/irritability and activation, with substantial impairment. IL-6 elevations are reported, but findings vary. The rapid-cycling study pooled mania and hypomania, preventing a separate hypomania estimate. [S3][S1]\n- **Hypomania:** less severe elevation/activation, without mania’s marked impairment. The clearest dedicated study associated childhood IL-6 with self-reported hypomanic features at 22: adjusted odds ratio 1.77 (95% CI 1.10–2.85), highest versus lowest third. This was observational—not confirmed bipolar diagnosis or treatment evidence. [S4][S1]\n- **Euthymia:** relatively stable mood outside episodes. Blood IL-6 can still be elevated; cerebrospinal-fluid findings differ. [S3]\n\n**Our review identified no convincing hypomania-specific IL-6 intervention benefit.** The dedicated cohort appraisal was abstract-based. [S1][S4]","chargedUpperUSD":1.6275249999999997}
{"turn":3,"elapsedMs":61380,"answer":"**Anxiety:** Panic-disorder meta-analyses report higher IL-6, but NESDA found no overall adjusted association with anxiety disorders. [S1][S2] A 77-person randomized generalized-anxiety psychotherapy trial found symptom improvement without inflammatory improvement; IL-6 increased in the acceptance-based arm. [S3]\n\n**PTSD:** Meta-analyses associate PTSD with higher IL-6, but estimates vary with trauma exposure among controls and other confounders. [S1] A 20-person double-blind, sham-controlled vagus-stimulation pilot improved symptoms and blocked stress-evoked IL-6 responses—not proof that IL-6 changes mediated benefit. Related reports are not independent replications. [S4][S1] Conversely, pooled mindfulness trials involving 210 veterans found no between-group difference in IL-6 change despite clinical improvement. [S5]\n\n**Suicidal ideation:** In veterans, IL-6 tracked ideation severity but did not predict whether ideation was present. [S6] A 2025 meta-analysis found higher IL-6 versus healthy controls, but no significant difference between depressed people with versus without suicidal behaviour, with extreme heterogeneity. [S7]\n\n**No—IL-6 is not a validated suicide-risk test.** Ideation, attempts and suicide deaths are distinct outcomes. Association does not establish individual prediction, and no randomized evidence here establishes suicide prevention through IL-6 lowering. [S1]\n\nThis is a narrative evidence map; most cited study appraisals were principally abstract-based. [S1]","chargedUpperUSD":2.4436749999999994}
{"turn":4,"elapsedMs":61853,"answer":"**No.** The expanded review establishes neither that IL-6 causes ADHD nor that lowering it treats ADHD. [S1]\n\n**Randomized evidence:** The eight-week omega-3 trial (103 children) reported reduced IL-6 and improved parent-rated symptoms, but parallel changes do not prove that IL-6 reduction caused improvement. [S2] Vitamin D did not significantly reduce IL-6 versus placebo after three months. [S3] In the probiotic pilot, IL-6 declined in both probiotic and placebo groups—not a treatment-specific effect. [S4] These supplement trials do not isolate IL-6 as the therapeutic mechanism.\n\n**Pooled evidence:** The 2021 meta-analysis found no significant IL-6 difference (p=0.09), which does not prove equivalence. [S5] The 2022 analysis found higher IL-6, but significance disappeared when individual studies were removed. [S6] The 2026 network meta-analysis highlighted elevated IL-6 in autism; ADHD signals concerned other markers. Autism findings cannot be transferred to ADHD. [S7]\n\nOverall, associations, biomarker changes and symptom benefits are separate findings. Several appraisals relied on abstracts; full-text assessment was limited. [S1]","chargedUpperUSD":3.167324999999999}
{"turn":5,"elapsedMs":71041,"answer":"- **Alcohol:** Diagnosed alcohol use disorder is associated with moderately higher IL-6, with drinking, withdrawal and health-related confounding. The larger **102-person, 12-week, double-blind ibudilast trial found no benefit on heavy drinking or peripheral inflammatory markers** versus placebo, limiting earlier pilot signals. [S1][S2]\n\n- **Opioids:** Higher IL-6 correlates with poorer methadone-treatment and cognitive outcomes. Confounding and reverse causation remain; intervention studies have not established an IL-6-mediated benefit. [S1]\n\n- **Stimulants:** Methamphetamine findings include elevated and unchanged IL-6. In cocaine research, the greatest use reduction accompanied increased IL-6—lower is not automatically better. [S1]\n\n- **Cannabis:** The retained study concerns cannabis exposure in people with and without HIV, **not diagnosed cannabis-use disorder**. Main associations involved other inflammatory markers, not demonstrated IL-6 treatment efficacy. [S3]\n\n- **Nicotine:** The retained evidence concerns cigarette smoking in older adults, **not isolated nicotine or diagnosed dependence**. Higher CRP and lower soluble IL-6 receptor indicate a mixed profile; the receptor is distinct from IL-6 itself. [S4]\n\n**Bottom line:** Exposure, diagnosed disorder, withdrawal and recovery are distinct. Biomarker associations or changes do not establish causation or clinical benefit. [S1]","chargedUpperUSD":3.875574999999999}
